  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will be in place Thursday and Friday. This is a \nresult of upper high pressure anchored across the southeast CONUS \nand an upper low across the Pacific northwest. This will allow a \nsurge of sub-tropical moisture to move northeastward into the area. \n09/18Z ENS/GEFS PWATS increase substantially Thursday and Thursday \nnight. Anomalously high values of 1.5 to 1.7 inches will be in \nplace. As a shortwave disturbance tracks northeastward across the \narea, surface low pressure deepens across northeast Colorado. A warm \nfront will arc eastward from the low. As mentioned, an increase mid-\nlevel sub-tropical moisture is expected, with an increase in low-\nlevel moisture as well. Locally heavy rainfall could become a \nconcern Thursday night, as a southerly low-level jet develops across \nKansas and noses into the warm front located across the southern \nNebraska vicinity. Elevated instability will increase north of the \nfront across much of western and north central Nebraska, with \nshowers and thunderstorms expected to develop. The 09/18Z ENS 24 \nmean QPF is exceeding 1\", with a bullseye located across the \nSandhills into north central Nebraska. POPs are high during this \ntimeframe and see no reason to lower with all the available moisture \nand warm frontal boundary set-up across the area.   \n\nThe upper level low across the Pacific northwest will fill and \ntranslate eastward across the Northern Plains Friday. Ridging aloft \nthen builds across the area during the weekend into the first of \nnext week. At the surface, a cold front will move southward through \nthe area Saturday with a cooler, drier Canadian airmass spreading \ninto the area Saturday through Monday. Highs will mostly be in the \n70s during this timeframe with only a slight chance for any \nprecipitation.",
